# Dependency rule

Source dependencies point inward. Outer code may call inner code. Inner code never imports outer code.

```
presentation / infrastructure
        ↓
   application
        ↓
     domain
```

| Layer | May import | Must not import |
| ----- | ---------- | --------------- |
| Domain | Pure language. No framework. | Application, infrastructure, presentation, widgets, generated clients |
| Application | Domain, and ports the use case owns | Repository implementations, endpoints, widgets, providers |
| Infrastructure or data | Domain, and the framework that stores or fetches | Application, presentation |
| Presentation | Application ports through the composition root, and domain types | Concrete `*RepositoryImpl`, the database, the generated client used as a domain type |
| Composition root (`app/`) | Every layer, to construct them | Business rules. No `if` that decides whether a title is valid |

Infrastructure and presentation are both outer. They do not import each other.

Three models meet at a boundary. The domain entity, the persistence row, and the wire payload are different types. Map at the edge. A blank title fails in the entity, before a repository is called.

The composition root is the only place that constructs a repository implementation. Endpoints, pages, and notifiers receive a use case.

Folder ownership is a separate rule. Copy one of `layer-first.mdc`, `feature-first.mdc`, or `hybrid.mdc` beside this file. Do not copy more than one layout.
